"""`CSS.supports` is missing: every feature-detecting script on the web (Modernizr, every framework's `CSS.supports("selector(:has(a))")`, `if (CSS.supports("display", "grid"))`) throws on its first line or takes the legacy branch. GOAL: cssom-1 §6.7 / css-conditional-3 §7: `CSS.supports(property, value)` → true iff the declaration `property: value` is supported (the SAME oracle @supports uses: `declaration_supported`: no second list; a custom property `--x` with any value is true; a value ending `!important` is false) and `CSS.supports(conditionText)` → the `supports_condition` parse-and-evaluate of the text (wrapped in parentheses when it is a bare declaration, per spec), including `selector()`; both return booleans and never throw for string arguments. If `CSS.escape` is absent, build it too (cssom-1 §2.1 serialize-an-identifier: ≤ 25 lines, one tail fn) — it is the other `CSS.*` every library calls.
M1 measure: everything above by fn and line; how typed_om.rs installs a native fn on `CSS` (the model); how @supports reaches the oracle from a parsed rule (the entry to call with a text); the argument coercion (ToString); what `CSS.supports("display")` (one arg, bare ident) returns in Chromium: false.
M2 build: typed_om.rs (or the file that owns `CSS`): two in-place install lines + ONE tail block with the native fns; css/mod.rs ONE pub(crate) accessor if the oracle is private.
M3 tests (engine_tests tail `css_supports_*`: `CSS.supports("display", "grid")` true (armed: today a throw); `("display", "bogus")` false; `("text-align-last", "justify")` true; `("--x", "anything")` true; `("color", "red !important")` false; one-arg `"(display: flex) and (not (display: bogus))"` true; `"display: flex"` true (the bare-declaration wrap); `"selector(:has(a))"` per the selector oracle's truth (state it); `CSS.supports("display")` false; typeof CSS.supports === "function"; `CSS.escape("a b#1")` === "a\\\\ b\\\\#1" and a leading digit escapes as `\\\\31 `; the unit factories unchanged (pin)).""",
'AETHER-CSSOM-CSS-REGISTERPROPERTY, AETHER-CSSOM-CSS-HIGHLIGHTS (the rest of the CSS namespace), AETHER-TEXT-JUSTIFY-WIDE carried.',
